1. LICENSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is a **dual-licensed repository**. The two layers carry different
licenses, and the boundary is the provenance boundary itself:

  * **Qianmo's own code -- GNU AGPL-3.0-or-later** (`LICENSE` at the root).
    This covers the `@qianmo/*` workspace packages under `packages/`, the
    residency modifications to the base runtime, the demo and acceptance
    suites under `demo/`, and documentation written by the Qianmo AgentNest
    Team (`docs/dev/` and others). The test is the **file header**: these 564
    files carry both `SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later` and
    `Copyright 2026 Qianmo AgentNest Team`.

  * **Code imported from the base project -- MIT License** (`LICENSE.base`,
    the upstream text kept verbatim). Base files carry **no SPDX header**,
    which is the machine-checkable test between the two layers:

        # the 564 files in the AGPL layer (restricted to the file header,
        # so documents that merely quote the identifier -- this file among
        # them -- are not counted)
        git grep -n "SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later" \
          | awk -F: '$2<=5' | cut -d: -f1 | sort -u

    See section 2 below.

  * **The exception has narrowed to the two Windows triples**: the six
    prebuilt `.node` files under `vendor/audio-capture/` previously had no
    source at all anywhere. **The source now lives in the repository**
    (`packages/audio-capture-napi/native/`, carrying the AGPL-3.0-or-later file
    header), and **any triple already replaced by a build from that source is
    no longer part of this exception** -- `arm64-darwin`, `x64-darwin`,
    `arm64-linux` and `x64-linux` have **all four been replaced and verified
    by real loading on their target architecture** (not a structural
    inference; see section 5 below). **Only `arm64-win32` and `x64-win32`
    remain unreplaced**: they still fall into **neither layer above** -- they
    carry no SPDX header, and the base MIT layer covers base code in source
    form. They are **accompanying binaries of undetermined license**; the
    per-triple status and the evidence are in section 5 below. This file does
    **not** label these two unreplaced binaries AGPL: AGPL requires
    Corresponding Source on distribution, and labelling a binary whose source
    we cannot ship would put this repository in violation of its own license.

**Why AGPL rather than GPL**: Qianmo's second product line is a communication
and collaboration network connecting resident agents. The most likely way for
it to be taken without reciprocity is for someone to run it as a hosted
service -- which GPL-3.0 does not reach and AGPL-3.0 section 13 (Remote
Network Interaction) does. For a network-shaped product, only AGPL is strict
in substance.

**The MIT-to-AGPL direction is permitted**: MIT is permissive and may be
incorporated into an AGPL work; the reverse is not true. Contributing this
repository's changes to the base layer back upstream (MIT) therefore requires
a separate grant. The base project and this repository share one owner, so
dual licensing is available, but it is **not automatic** and is decided case
by case. Per roadmap v2.41 the current position on upstreaming is "not for
now, revisit individually".

**This change is not retroactive**: the repository was distributed under MIT
before this change. Copies obtained under MIT keep that grant; AGPL applies
only to versions published after this change.

**Section 13 binds us too**: AGPL-3.0 section 13 requires an operator to offer
Corresponding Source to users who interact with the program over a network.
Qianmo's console and its resident nodes are exactly that, **including the
deployments we run ourselves**. This is the one ongoing obligation the
relicensing adds, and it is not a clause that only applies to other people.

  Note: earlier revisions of this repository (commit 67f6081 and prior) used the
  Apache License 2.0. That decision was superseded by the base-adoption change;
  see docs/dev/charter.md §5.5.

